{"name":"Helen of Troy","hex":"#c3b89f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"helen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Either of a pair of curved, spine-like appendages on some hyoliths."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/helen"},{"term":"troy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, or relating to, troy weight."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/troy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Helen of Troy","blocks":[{"text":"Helen, commonly known as Helen of Troy and sometimes as Helen of Sparta, is a figure in Greek mythology said to have been the most beautiful woman in the world. She was believed to have been the daughter of Zeus by Leda or Nemesis, and the sister of Clytemnestra, Castor, Pollux, Philonoe, Phoebe and Timandra. She was first married to King Menelaus of Sparta \"who became by her the father of Hermione, and, according to others, of Nicostratus also.\" Her subsequent marriage to Paris of Troy was the most immediate cause of the Trojan War."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Helen_of_Sparta_boards_a_ship_for_Troy_fresco_from_the_House_of_the_Tragic_Poet_in_Pompeii.jpg/330px-Helen_of_Sparta_boards_a_ship_for_Troy_fresco_from_the_House_of_the_Tragic_Poet_in_Pompeii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helen_of_Troy","wikidata":"Q164061"},{"title":"Helen of Troy (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Helen of Troy is a 1956 American-Italian-French epic historical drama film, based on Homer's Iliad and Odyssey. It was directed by Robert Wise, from a screenplay by Hugh Gray and John Twist, adapted by Hugh Gray and N. Richard Nash. The music score was composed by Max Steiner and the cinematography by Harry Stradling Sr, who shot the film in CinemaScope. The movie has been called \"a forgotten Robert Wise epic\"."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/90/Helen_of_Troy_1956_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helen_of_Troy_(film)","wikidata":"Q938924"},{"title":"Troy","blocks":[{"text":"Troy or Ilion was an ancient city located in present-day Çanakkale, Turkey. It is best known as the setting for the Greek myth of the Trojan War. The modern-day archaeological site, also known as Hisarlık, is open to the public as a tourist destination, and was added to the UNESCO World Heritage list in 1998."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Walls_of_Troy_%282%29.jpg/330px-Walls_of_Troy_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Troy","wikidata":"Q22647"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Les_Amours_de_P%C3%A2ris_et_d%27H%C3%A9l%C3%A8ne_%28painting_by_Jacques-Louis_David%29.jpg/500px-Les_Amours_de_P%C3%A2ris_et_d%27H%C3%A9l%C3%A8ne_%28painting_by_Jacques-Louis_David%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Les_Amours_de_P%C3%A2ris_et_d%27H%C3%A9l%C3%A8ne_%28painting_by_Jacques-Louis_David%29.jpg/1280px-Les_Amours_de_P%C3%A2ris_et_d%27H%C3%A9l%C3%A8ne_%28painting_by_Jacques-Louis_David%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Les_Amours_de_P%C3%A2ris_et_d%27H%C3%A9l%C3%A8ne_%28painting_by_Jacques-Louis_David%29.jpg/500px-Les_Amours_de_P%C3%A2ris_et_d%27H%C3%A9l%C3%A8ne_%28painting_by_Jacques-Louis_David%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Love of Helen and Paris by Jacques-Louis David (oil on canvas, 1788, Louvre, Paris)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALes_Amours_de_P%C3%A2ris_et_d'H%C3%A9l%C3%A8ne_(painting_by_Jacques-Louis_David).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Red-figure_cup_Birth_of_Helen_%28Boston_MFA_99.539%29.jpg/500px-Red-figure_cup_Birth_of_Helen_%28Boston_MFA_99.539%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Red-figure_cup_Birth_of_Helen_%28Boston_MFA_99.539%29.jpg/1280px-Red-figure_cup_Birth_of_Helen_%28Boston_MFA_99.539%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Red-figure_cup_Birth_of_Helen_%28Boston_MFA_99.539%29.jpg/500px-Red-figure_cup_Birth_of_Helen_%28Boston_MFA_99.539%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The birth of Helen on an Athenian red-figure cup by the Xenotimos Painter, c. 430–420 BC. The egg, watched over by the eagle of Zeus, rests on an altar flanked by Tyndareus and Leda; behind Tyndareus stands Clytemnestra.[35]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARed-figure_cup_Birth_of_Helen_(Boston_MFA_99.539).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Leda_and_the_Swan_1505-1510.jpg/500px-Leda_and_the_Swan_1505-1510.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Leda_and_the_Swan_1505-1510.jpg/500px-Leda_and_the_Swan_1505-1510.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Leda and the Swan by Cesare da Sesto (c. 1506–1510, Wilton). The artist has been intrigued by the idea of Helen's unconventional birth; she and Clytemnestra are shown emerging from one egg; Castor and Pollux from another.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALeda_and_the_Swan_1505-1510.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Theseus_pursuit_Louvre_G423.jpg/500px-Theseus_pursuit_Louvre_G423.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_c